= Semantic network — Technical =

== Graph representation ==
A semantic network can be represented:

<pre>
N = (V, E, λ)
</pre>

where:
* V = concept/entity nodes;
* E = edges;
* λ labels edges with semantic relation types.

This graph form alone does not determine a logic.

== Inheritance networks ==
Hierarchical networks may implement inheritance:

<pre>
A isA B
B hasProperty P
→ A inherits P
</pre>

Default inheritance requires exception handling.

Non-monotonic networks can retract inherited conclusions when a more specific node supplies an exception.

== Spreading activation ==
A network can attach activation a(v) to nodes.

Activation update may depend on neighboring nodes and edge weights:

<pre>
a_t+1(v) = decay(a_t(v)) + Σ w_uv a_t(u)
</pre>

Specific models differ in normalization, thresholds and inhibition.

This is associative retrieval, not logical entailment.

== Semantic distance ==
Path length or weighted connectivity can approximate conceptual similarity.

But graph distance reflects modeling decisions and does not automatically correspond to psychological or lexical similarity.

== Formalization ==
Some later KR systems gave semantic networks precise logical interpretations by translating nodes and links into first-order or description-logic expressions.

This improves inference guarantees but changes the original informal flexibility.

